  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 233 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A Nor'easter centered off the Mid-Atlantic coast will be \nthe main weather feature this weekend, with our region on the \nperiphery of the system and more impactful weather further south \ntowards the coast. Several rain bands will move across southern \nVermont and try to make their way further north, but drier air \nacross the region will limit their progress and how much \nprecipitation is expected. Throughout the weekend, southern Vermont \nwill see the most precipitation after several rounds of showers, but \nfurther north look to remain drier. The system will slowly exit the \nregion later this weekend into the start of next week, bringing \ngreater precipitation chances for Sunday into Monday before drier \nconditions return towards Tuesday. \n\nIn addition to the precipitation, a strong jet looks to bring gusty \nwinds to the region, particularly tonight into Sunday, with the \ngreatest concern across the western slopes in southern Vermont and \nsummit levels. Temperatures will be on the cooler side as well, \nespecially for Sunday and Monday with clouds and periods of showers \nexpected, especially across southern and eastern Vermont.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: No impactful weather is expected for the middle to later \nportions of next week. The Nor'easter currently affecting southern \nNew England and the Mid-Atlantic region will exist in a much \nweakened state by Tuesday night and be in the process of exiting \nnortheast into the westerlies away from our region as skies trend \npartly cloudy. Thereafter, weak high pressure mainly controls \nregional weather conditions for the Wed-Thu time frame with daily \nhighs warming into the upper 60s to lower 70s. This morning's GFS \ndepiction of a rather sharp northern stream trough feature and \nscattered/numerous showers passing through the area Wednesday \nevening appears overdone. This solution has little support from \nother output which seems reasonable given weak baroclinicity evident \nin 925-850 hPa thermal profiles and I've offered mainly a dry \nweather through this time frame. By Friday a somewhat more \npronounced cold front appears poised to swing through the region. \nAgain, moisture is rather starved with this feature, so \nprecipitation chances will be generally held in the 20-40% range. \nBehind the front, slightly cooler air arrives for next weekend, but \nvalues should remain within 5 degrees of seasonal early October \nnorms.",
